[#:seon.task{:name "a-check-step-is-a-task",
             :plan
             "What exists: bin/check's steps run as a script (`seon.check`), beside the reconciler. Correct: each step is a task in resources/seon/tasks/seon.check.edn carrying :seon.task/argv and :needs; the reconciler runs it as a child through one effect owner (`seon.fx`, an exclusive step running alone); its outcome is the run's end facts (exit, signal, output path); the run ends on the quiet fact and names the red step with what it blocks. One machinery for agents and checks; replaces the check script's own ordering. Shown in a prototype (e7_one_system): 9 steps in 1.46 s against 1.9 s serial, exclusive steps never overlapped. A first implementation on an older base is jolt/runner b4ea59d (reference only: :check tasks, an :fx/proc child owner, deftests a-red-check-step-blocks-only-its-dependents, a-check-step-passes-by-its-own-expected-exit, an-exclusive-child-runs-alone).",
             :kind :design,
             :needs
             #{"clojure.core.async.flow/a-graph-counts-its-messages-and-says-when-it-is-quiet"},
             :proofs
             #{seon.check-test/check-steps-run-by-needs-and-end-on-quiet}}
 {:seon.task/name "contract-gaps-have-one-rule"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the turn loop takes the contract gaps the branch adds since its merge base (`seon.turn/added-gaps`, over `seon.merge/uncontracted`), records them on the run (:seon.run/uncontracted), and root's judge reads that record (`seon.reconcile/done-input`, `seon.reconcile/done-explain`); the context reads the judge's errors (`seon.context`). :gaps-before is gone. Owed: the proof that the loop, the context and the judge name the same gaps, including a gap main gained after the branch point and a var re-evaluated unchanged."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/the-loop-the-context-and-the-judge-name-the-same-gaps}}
 {:seon.task/name "render-reads-the-reconcilers-index"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.render.proc/main-info` rebuilds from main's db, on every main commit, what the reconciler already keeps incrementally: `seon.reconcile/index`, `seon.task/tasks`, the runs, `seon.problems/report` and a datom count. Measured on a root of 92 tasks, 13 runs, 1,820 datoms: 113 ms per main commit (index 58 ms, tasks 26 ms, runs 8 ms, report 5 ms), against 0.87 ms for a quiet render pass. Correct: the reconciler emits each task row (state, blocked-by, proofs, runs, tokens), each run and the recorded problems as it changes them, and render reads those events; render's task rows, its index rebuild and its per-commit task and run reads go. Estimate: work per main commit in proportion to the tasks and runs that commit touched."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/render-rebuilds-no-index-per-commit
                      seon.render-test/render-main-commit-cost}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refused-spawn-is-recorded"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: when root refuses a spawn (`seon.root` answers :spawn with :seon.root/refused: the branch is held by a live agent, or `:seon.config.root/max-agents` are live), the reconciler's :spawned step in `seon.reconcile/advance` writes the error as a run through `seon.task/run-start-tx`, status :refused, no agent (:seon.run/agent is optional), root's message as :seon.run/reason and the error map; `seon.reconcile/root-causes` names a task whose latest run is refused as a root cause. A refused task waits in the proc's :refused until main moves for another reason (`seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc`'s :main clears it on a commit that is not only refused runs): asking again at once was a hot loop that grew main without bound. Owed elsewhere: `seon.e2e/agents-reused` counts runs by agent and would count a refused one's absent agent."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-refused-spawn-writes-a-refused-run
                      seon.reconcile-test/quiescence-names-a-task-that-cannot-start
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-refused-task-asks-again-when-main-moves}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refused-flight-write-is-not-ignored"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: every flight's write (:spawned, :started, :ran, :octopus-merged, then :done) goes through the writer, and the :done step of `seon.reconcile/advance` is `(done st)`: it drops the answer. Reproduction (jolt/runner e0efb69): the store hit MDB_MAP_FULL, the writer refused a run's end (`seon.writer/writer-one` answers :seon.writer/refused), the run stayed :running, the reconciler never reached quiet and nothing said why. Correct: an answer that is an error map is read: the flight records it where it can (the run's :seon.run/error by a smaller write, or the reconciler's state and ping-map as :refused with the run and message when main refuses every write), and quiescence counts the flight as ended with that error, so the run ends and names the error. The refused spawn (above) covers spawns only. A first fix on :check tasks is jolt/runner b880791 (WIP); its proof is re-targeted to an agent's run."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-refused-end-write-is-reported-at-quiet}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-octopus-merge-is-proven-by-one-combined-run"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: octopus merges (the reconciler's :octopus-merge op) and reach selection (`seon.agent/run-tests-forked`), kept off by seon.reconcile/octopus-merges-off-as-decided until this exists. Correct: an octopus merge's members are proven together by one combined run of the tests their changes reach; on red the members are bisected (halves re-run) to name the one that broke it, and the green members merge. Replaces the off switch. Reference only: jolt/r5-port 302ce17 (parked, on an old base; notes/archive/option3-R5-octopus merges.md); re-derive on main's reconciler."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-red-octopus-merge-is-bisected-to-its-member}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-outside-runs-agent-death-ends-it"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an outside run (`seon.server/attach!`, model \"outside\") is written by the server, and a reconciler-started outside run's flight ends at :started (`seon.reconcile/advance`), so nothing observes its agent's death: the run stays :running with its agent :dead, counts against :max-running, and a waiter (a check step, a test) hangs. A model run's death is observed only as the answer to its flight's pending eval (`seon.reconcile/dead-tx`). Root records the death on main as a problem (`seon.root/record-death`), but no fact names the run. Correct: an agent's death is a fact root writes on main with the agent's id when it sees the agent end, and the reconciler ends every :running run whose agent died through `dead-tx`, outside or not. Replaces the flight-only death path. Confirmed again by step E's dry run (2026-10-22): an outside agent killed (SIGKILL; it ignored SIGTERM, which the fork fix on jolt/fork-carriers explains) left its run `:running` with no agent, and the task never got another run until `seon_attach`. Two more facts of an outside run to fix with it: its `:seon.run/pid` is nil (the agent's process is found only through the process tree), and its outcome is `:no-end` even after the agent called `(my.turn/wait)` (the stop it requested is never written as its end, so the judge and the problems row read a waiting agent as one that wrote no end)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/an-outside-run-ends-dead-when-its-agent-dies}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-reconciler-reads-config-and-one-graph-walk"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the reconciler reads its limits through the one resolver (`seon.config/value`, task -> model -> provider -> config): `seon.reconcile/actions` merges main's root fact over :seon.config.agent/max-running and :seon.config.merge/max-running; its own default defs are deleted. The transitive walk over :needed-by is one private `seon.reconcile/downstream` (`seon.program/topo` stays apart: it orders program keys depth first). A run's id is derived once, `seon.task/run-id`. Not done, by root's ruling: routing `seon.server/attach!` through the spawn flight (it would undo the one-transaction attach, seon.server/attach-writes-nothing-when-it-is-refused); `seon.root/start!`'s options are task seon.root/start-takes-a-config-layer. The test oracle (test/r3/oracle.clj) keeps its own copies on purpose: it is the independent full computation the incremental one is checked against."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/max-running-caps-parallel-runs seon.reconcile-test/boot-records-merge-max-running-one r3.inc-test/incremental-equals-full}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-design-is-done-when-its-proofs-are-designed-red"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.reconcile/done-explain` judges a :design run only by its plan changing (:no-design). Reproduction (agent-work audit): 4 of 4 designs wrote full bodies and no cost proof, and two passed as done while registering a 3-value status enum over 10 stored values. Correct (DESIGN-tasks §7, prototyped): design is done when a task needing it holds at least one proof and each such proof is designed red: every report :pass or :fail, or an :error whose ex-data carries :seon.error/type (a stub's :seon.error/unimplemented), with at least one not a pass. An untyped error (No such var) or an all-green proof is not designed red: error :not-designed-red naming the proof. Deletes: nothing; :no-design stays."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.task/design-writes-the-implement-steps-proofs"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-design-is-refused-until-its-proofs-are-designed-red}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-unreached-change-refuses-the-last-task"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.reconcile/done-explain` refuses red, unknown and stray tests, regressions, contract gaps and unmerged entries, but not a change no test reaches. v1's done rule refused 'functions no green test covers' at the last task of a chain, and its `my.test/check` answered :seon.test/unreached. Correct: at a task that carries :seon.task/target (the merge), every var in `seon.program/divergence` against the branch's base that no green test's reach covers (the reach `seon.proofs/plan` selects by, over `seon.program/reach-digests`) refuses {:error :uncovered :symbol s}; the context names each with the form that lists what reaches it. Deletes nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/an-unreached-change-refuses-at-the-merge}}
 {:seon.task/name "octopus-merges-off-as-decided"
  :seon.task/plan "Restates a ruling now in force (DECISIONS: Root's calls on R3's questions: octopus merges stay off until one combined test run of all members, the par-200 hang and root memory growth with concurrent merges are fixed): `:seon.config.merge/max-running` is 1 in resources/seon/config/default.edn, read through the one resolver, and `seon.task/boot-tx` writes it on main with the config in force; the code default `seon.reconcile/default-max-merging` is deleted. Nothing left to do: close."
