 {:seon.task/name "an-observed-retraction-leaves-no-residue"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by worktree session context-render-observes-what-changed (2026-10-24): `seon.reconcile/observe` of a retraction answers an index unequal to `seon.reconcile/index` of the same db. Retracting run demo/a#1 leaves `:by-task {\"demo/a\" #{}}`; retracting task demo/d leaves `:acc`, `:depth` and `:done` entries for demo/d and empty `:needed-by` sets for its needs (`put-run`, `put-task`, `derive-needs` and `refresh-done` move entries but never dissoc a gone one). `:tasks`, `:claims` and the ready set are right, so actions and the done rule read the same answers; the residue grows with retractions. Reproduction: seon.reconcile-test/an-index-observed-from-the-last-is-the-index-listed with the steps [[:db/retractEntity [:seon.run/id \"demo/a#1\"]]] and [[:db/retractEntity [:seon.task/id \"demo/d\"]]] after its new boot. Correct: a gone entity's entries are dissoc'd where they are moved (the owner of each map), so an observed index equals the listed one. Proof (to write, red today): those two retractions observed equal the index listed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/an-observed-retraction-leaves-no-residue}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-work-is-tasks"
  :seon.task/plan "PLANNED, NOT BUILT: waits for root's go and for the agent loop's park and send parts (design `notes/design-agent-loop-2026-10-25.md` on jolt/agent-loop, build parts 3-4). Owner, 2026-10-25 (docs/decisions.md: root is main; root's special cases go): root's own work is tasks run by agents on main's current commit, never a human-only sink.

What exists. A failure is `seon.reconcile/hold-tx`: the task `:held` with `:seon.task/failure {:reason :run}` and `:seon.task/evidence` (`seon.reconcile/evidence`: conflicts, unmerged, reds, problem), the run `:closed`. Its causes are `seon.reconcile/hold-tx`'s callers: `seon.reconcile/actions`' `failed` (the done rule refused, or the agent died) and the `:octopus-merged` step (a merge refused other than `:behind`). Nobody owns a held task: `seon.reconcile/root-causes` lists them, and `quiet` writes that list into ONE fact, `:seon.root/quiescent` on the root entity, which every later quiet spell overwrites, so a cause nobody read before the next spell is gone. Three more root failures are not even holds: a red proof on main (`seon.prove/result-tx`'s `:seon.proof/latest`, read by `seon.problems` as `:test-red`), a health problem a merge caused on main (`seon.problems/caused`, judged per branch before its merge, never on the octopus's combined result), and a failed build (`:seon.root/generation` `{:error}`, again one overwritten fact). Templates exist only for work kinds (`seon.template/instructions`: merge, design, implement, test).

Which failures become tasks, given the agent loop. Under the loop design a `:waiting`, `:cap`, `:no-progress` or provider-`:error` end parks with no hold, and a refused done-rule end holds and parks with its reason in the user's timeline (the loop's owner question 2): both stay the session's own, not root's. Root's are the five the owner named: a refused merge, a dead agent, health a merge caused, red proofs on main, a failed update.

The change (one mechanism, `seon.template`'s request path reused).
1. Five templates in `seon.template/instructions`, kinds `:root-merge :agent-died :health :red-proofs :update`, task namespace `root`, model and budgets from config like any task (`seon.reconcile/desired-env`): no kind switch in the reconciler; the agent's instructions say what to read and what ends it.
2. One new function in seon.template, root-task, from a failure (kind, the evidence map, the failed task and run, or main's t for health/red/update) to one task declaration. Its id is the cause's own: `root/merge-<run>`, `root/died-<run>`, `root/health-<main t>`, `root/red-<test>@<digest>`, `root/update-<commit>`. So the same failure seen twice is the same task (an upsert of identical facts) and two failures are two tasks: nothing overwrites.
3. Each producer writes the task in the SAME transaction as the fact of the failure: `hold-tx` (merge refused, agent died) gains the task's datoms; `seon.prove`'s result write for main gains a `:red-proofs` task when a test turned red at a digest that was green; the octopus merge's `:committed` write gains a `:health` task when `seon.problems/caused` of main after vs before is non-empty (cost to measure first: the per-branch `:caused` already runs in each judge); `my.root/build!`'s generation write gains an `:update` task on `{:error}`. One write per failure, as `request-tx` writes a linked set.
4. The task runs like any task: the reconciler spawns its agent forked from main's committed head (main's current program: the fork server holds it, `program/load-changes!`), on its own branch `root/<id>` (its writes, if any, merge by its own merge task, `with-merge`, through the one merge path). A root task's agent never holds another session's branch.
5. How each relates to the failed task (the parked agent of the loop design is never killed by it):
   - `:root-merge`: the failed merge task is held and its agent parked on its branch. The root task's agent reads the evidence and the branch (read-only: `my.branch` reads of another branch), then acts through the one send: a message to that session (wakes it, with the conflicts and what to do) or `my.task/fail!` of the original with a reason. Messaging a held task from root releases it in the same transaction (loop design section 3). The root task ends done when it has sent its decision; the original's own next run is judged as any run.
   - `:agent-died`: the run is `:dead` and the session has no process (loop design: the next message resumes it at its head). The root task reads exit status, stderr tail and `last-op`; it resumes the session by a message, or files a platform task (a defect in what died), or fails the original.
   - `:health`, `:red-proofs`: work tasks on main's current commit: make the problem rows or the reds go away, proved by `seon.problems/caused` empty and the reds green; their fix merges by the merge task like any work.
   - How a `:health` task closes when its cause is gone: no new rule; its proof is the problem row it names reading `:open? false` or being absent (`seon.problems/closed?`: a fact written on the error's subject after its last occurrence). So a row a live proc makes untrue closes the task without an agent's fix: the reconciler's observe sees the proof true and the task done, and the spawn it would have made is not made. That needs every recorded error to name a subject whose later write means its cause is gone. Today a `:platform` error has none: a past boot's `:seon.watch/died` stays open while this boot's watcher is alive (task `seon.problems/a-platform-error-closes-when-its-cause-is-gone`), which merges first. A health task for a row with no such subject is the agent's to judge: it reads the cause, and ends the task either with a fix or with `my.task/fail!` and its reason.
   - `:update`: the build's error is its evidence; fix on main as work, or the build input that failed.
6. No recursion: a root task's own failure (its merge refused, its agent dead) is held and parked for the user, with no root task of a root task (the template reads the failed task's ns: `root` gets none). That is the one human sink left, at the top.
7. Deleted: the `:root-causes` member of `:seon.root/quiescent` and its write in `quiet` (the quiet spell keeps its `{:t}` fact: the signal tests and `seon.await` wait on, an event, not a store of causes); `seon.reconcile/root-causes` stays only as the render of root's page (the open `root/*` tasks and the held tasks that block released work), reading tasks, storing nothing; the `{:error}` overwrite of `:seon.root/generation` stays as the build's answer, the task is its record.

Correctness: every failure is a durable task with an owner, a run, proofs and an end; two failures keep two causes; nothing a later spell writes erases one. Perf: one write per failure (the task's datoms ride the failure's own transaction); the health check of a merge is its one added cost, to measure (estimate: one `seon.problems/caused` over main before and after the octopus, which the judge already does per branch). Lines: about +120 (templates, root-task, producers) and -40 (root-causes storage, quiescent's causes): a capability, net up, complexity down.

Proofs (to write red first): two failures in a row (a refused merge, then a dead agent, with a quiet spell between and after) keep both causes, each as a `root/*` task, after every later quiet spell; the same failure observed twice is one task; a red proof on main makes one `:red-proofs` task naming the test and digest; a root task's own failure makes no root task; a root task's agent forks from main's head at its spawn (its program digest equals main's), on its own branch.

Questions for root: (a) admission: do root tasks take places under `:seon.config.agent/max-running` like any task, or go first (a config key, the owner's gate)? Recommended: like any task, ordered first in `actions`' starts. (b) does a `:root-merge` message the parked session (recommended: the session has the context) or work on its branch itself (needs the branch free)? (c) health on main per merge (cost measured first) or only when bin/prove's health step reds on main (cheaper, later)?"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/two-failures-in-a-row-keep-both-causes-as-tasks
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-failure-seen-twice-is-one-task
                      seon.prove-test/a-red-proof-on-main-is-a-task
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-root-tasks-failure-makes-no-root-task
                      seon.agent-test/a-root-task-forks-from-mains-head
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-health-task-closes-when-its-row-closes}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-scratch-function-is-no-contract-gap"
  :seon.task/kind :design
  :seon.task/plan "Build only if scratch contract gaps recur (root, 2026-10-26). What exists: the done rule refuses a run for each function its branch added without a contract (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`'s :contract-gap, from :seon.run/uncontracted, which `seon.turn/added-gaps` takes over every defn the branch adds since its merge base), whatever the function is for. Evidence (notes/live-baselines-2026-10-26.md; the thinking-off short-history designs, main cff9d6b9f): `(in-ns 'ns.x-test)` for the deftest left the REPL in the test namespace, the agent sketched the fix there, and the runs ended on its sketches: a \"contract-gap seon.context-test/cut3; contract-gap seon.context-test/cut4; contract-gap seon.context-test/kept-fixed; contract-gap seon.context-test/kept-fixed2; contract-gap seon.context-test/parts-of\", c \"no-design; contract-gap seon.context/kept-sketch\"; after jolt/design-plan-after-proof (the plan follows the red proof, no fix tried) the three reruns ended on none. Correct: a contract gap is judged on the functions the task's plan cites and the functions its proofs reach, the write set the one reference parser already derives (docs/references.md), not every defn on the branch; a scratch function elsewhere is the agent's, never a reason. Deletes nothing; changes which symbols `seon.turn/added-gaps` reads."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-scratch-function-is-no-contract-gap}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-observe-descends-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Observing main's index across a turn (```seon.reconcile/index``` with the index of an earlier db, seon.turn's index!) takes a datahike.api/datoms-diff of each of the 84 watched attributes' :aevt ranges, and on a stored db each is one cached descent from both roots, about 5-11k ticks and 30-60 KB where its attribute did not change, so the observe's fixed part is the attribute count's: measured on LMDB (seon.reconcile-test/observed-input), 1.11M ticks and 5.8 MB at 10 tasks, 1.03M and 4.6 MB at 500, 1.47M and 5.8 MB at 1,000, against about 30k for the one changed datom. One walk over the 84 ranges (a multi-prefix datoms-diff in our Datahike, on a jolt/<topic> branch) would descend once and skip the gaps between ranges: about 1M ticks a turn saved. Low priority (root, 2026-10-27): the proof now measures the stored shape, which is flat. Agent-loop measured a one-walk version for seon.program/diff-plan at 2.06 MB against 2.00 MB per attribute there (its cost was a comparator's try, since fixed), so measure this caller first. The other datoms-diff proofs already measure on a stored store (seon.program-cost-test/on-disk-diverged-of, seon.merge-cost-test/three-of); none measured on the test store states a reason that only holds on a stored one."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/an-index-is-observed-at-the-cost-of-what-changed}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-work-task-merges-itself-through-the-one-gate" :seon.task/plan "A work task merges itself through the one gate: the stand-alone parts of the merge design (notes/merge-audit-2026-10-27.md, Design; its independent review notes/merge-design-review-2026-10-27.md, section 5: worth it, merge these first). (a) Owner, 2026-10-27: \"drop with-merge from task files; a work task merges itself through the one gate. Keep design-build and stages as experiment templates\". `my.root/task-files` applies `seon.template/with-merge` to every file task, a merge task beside each work task: stop it, and let a green work task's run become :ready-to-merge as a whole task's does (`seon.task/merges?`). `seon.template/design-build` and `seon.template/stages` keep with-merge for experiments only. Measure the task count before and after (434 task entries in resources/seon/tasks on 2026-10-27; with-merge roughly doubles what load-tasks! writes). (b) Kept, not deleted (owner, 2026-10-27: \"we haven't used the merge code because our context was shit and agents didn't have instructions. Don't throw out the baby with the bathwater.\"): Seon's merge path stays whole, the octopus merge, the gate (`my.root/gate!`, :seon.task/gate?) and the merge tasks' machinery. The review's finding stands as a fact to fix, not a reason to delete: no local.edn or bin script sets :seon.config.root/gate-checkout, so the gate has never run on the standing root. (c) :seon.config.source/branch: default.edn names jolt/seon-merges, config/local.edn names jolt/merges. The review: the 14 Seon merge commits are only on jolt/seon-merges, written by bin/root roots in worktrees (bin/root sets SEON_SOURCE_ROOT and a worktree's local.edn names no branch) into the shared git dir; with source/root unset nothing is written. Decide which is right (the merges tree's branch is the one bin/update reads), make default.edn and local.edn agree, and stop worktree roots writing into the shared refs. So this task is (a) and (c) only. Not this task: routing Claude worktrees through the store, which waits on seon.merge/an-outside-branch-hands-back-through-the-store; those experiments go on, since they say whether the git side can come through Seon's path later." :seon.task/kind :work :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/a-green-work-task-merges-itself seon.template-test/task-files-make-no-merge-tasks}}]
